From 1e2522f589c00eb99d800543ffb21cadbd9d00a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rein Halbersma <rhalbersma@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 18:00:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [libc++] Implement P3104R6: Bit permutations

Adds std::bit_repeat, std::bit_reverse, std::bit_compress and
std::bit_expand to <bit> in C++29 mode, and bumps __cpp_lib_bitops to
202607L.

All four work on 64-bit blocks, so they cover every unsigned integer
type, unsigned __int128 and unsigned _BitInt(N) of any width, including
widths that are not a multiple of 64. bit_reverse uses
__builtin_bitreverse64 where available. bit_compress and bit_expand use
PEXT and PDEP outside constant evaluation where the target has fast BMI2
(spelled __builtin_elementwise_pext/pdep where Clang has them,
__builtin_ia32_pext_di/pdep_di otherwise), and leave them out on bdver4,
znver1 and znver2, where they are microcoded; LLVM still selects PEXT for
the elementwise builtin on those targets. Elsewhere, and in constant
evaluation, a portable kernel answers: compress places each selected bit
by one popcount of the mask below it, expand walks the mask's ones
without branching on the source.

The portable kernels and the block composition come from xstd-ints
(https://github.com/rhalbersma/xstd-ints), where they are proved equal to
the Intel SDM's PEXT/PDEP pseudocode for every 32- and 64-bit input with
Z3, swept exhaustively over all 16-bit pairs, and fuzzed.

Fixes #204404

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01HVg61PqZMJHpeJmibxQyDV
